<header
	transition:name="page-title"
	class="rounded-2xl bg-rose-200 px-6 py-8 text-center font-serif text-zinc-900"
>
	<h1 class="text-3xl font-bold">Privacy</h1>
</header>

<div class="mx-auto prose mt-8 max-w-3xl space-y-3 text-zinc-700 md:space-y-6">
	<p class="text-xl text-yellow-700">
		I'm Neil Hughes, and I barely understand privacy notices at the best of
		times, so I'll try my hardest to keep this simple.
	</p>
	<p>
		I am essentially just a person with a couple of simple websites, so perhaps
		having a full-blown Privacy Policy is overkill. Nevertheless, I believe I
		ought to do my reasonable best to be open and transparent when it comes to
		your data.
	</p>

	<p>
		This notice applies to{' '}<a
			class="text-link"
			href="https://enhughesiasm.com">enhughesiasm.com</a
		>,{' '}<a class="text-link" href="https://www.walkingoncustard.com"
			>walkingoncustard.com</a
		>, my mailing list, and the small projects I publish at{' '}<a
			class="text-link"
			href="https://enhughesiasm.github.io">enhughesiasm.github.io</a
		>.
	</p>

	<div class="space-y-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-100 px-8 pt-1 pb-6 md:px-12">
		<h3>The short version</h3>
		<p>
			<strong class="text-lg text-green-700"
				>Fundamentally, I'm not very interested in your data.</strong
			>
		</p>
		<p>
			I aim to gather as little as possible, and so I can't easily misuse it.
		</p>
		<p>
			But if you contact me or join my mailing list then, of course, I receive
			the information you choose to provide. A few third-party services also
			help the site work; they're listed below.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>That's about it!</strong> The rest of the page restates this in{
				' '
			}
			<em>slightly</em> more detail. Read on, if you're interested...
		</p>
	</div>

	<h3>The long version</h3>
	<p>
		While I have done some reading around this, I can't promise I understand
		everything about good data practices—I suspect it takes many years to become
		an expert in this sort of thing—but this document represents my best
		understanding of how your data is collected and used.
	</p>
	<p>
		If I discover that something has changed—or that a feature is silently doing
		more than I realised—I will update this notice. If you think I've missed
		something, please{' '}<a
			class="text-link"
			href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}contact`}
			><strong>let me know</strong></a
		>.
	</p>

	<h3>Who is responsible for your data?</h3>
	<p>
		I am: Neil Hughes. You can contact me at{' '}
		<strong>neil@walkingoncustard.com</strong> with privacy questions or requests.
	</p>

	<h3>What I collect and why</h3>

	<h4>If you contact me</h4>
	<p>
		If you email me or use the contact form (when it's not broken), I receive
		your name, email address, message, and any other information you include. I
		use it to read and reply to your message. (This is just… how email works,
		but for thoroughness I thought I ought to mention it.)
	</p>
	<p>
		My email is managed using Gmail, so any messages received are stored by
		Google.
	</p>
	<p>
		The contact form is often broken, but when it is functioning it uses Google
		reCAPTCHA to reduce spam. This means that Google may receive technical
		information about your browser and interaction with the site for that
		purpose.
	</p>

	<h4>If you join the Occasional Email Experience</h4>
	<p>
		I have an irregular email newsletter. I hate being sent too many emails, so
		I try not to send very many myself. It goes out at a very variable rate,
		from “once a month, ish” to “after a gap of a year or few".
	</p>
	<p>
		The newsletter is optional and double opt-in: after signing up, you have to
		click the confirmation link in your email. Mailchimp stores the information
		you provide—normally your email address and, optionally, your name—and sends
		the newsletter on my behalf. I use this information only to send and manage
		the newsletter you requested.
	</p>
	<p>
		Mailchimp also supplies delivery, opening, and link-click statistics.
		(Usually, I just use this to find out which subject-line puns were SO bad
		that more people unsubscribed than usual.)
	</p>
	<p>
		You can unsubscribe using the link in every newsletter or by emailing me.
	</p>
	<p></p>

	<h4>When you browse the site</h4>
	<p>
		The site is statically hosted. Like most hosting providers, the host may
		process basic technical information—such as IP address, browser details,
		requested page, and time of access—to deliver and secure the site. I do not
		currently use Google Analytics or another visitor analytics service.
	</p>
	<p>
		Some pages contain privacy-enhanced YouTube embeds. YouTube may receive
		technical data and may set or read cookies when you interact with a video.
		The Buy Me a Coffee button and widget are also provided by a third party. If
		you interact with either service, its own privacy terms apply.
	</p>
	<p>
		The site also links to external websites. Following a link takes you to a
		separate service with its own privacy practices.
	</p>

	<h3>Lawful bases</h3>
	<p>I rely on:</p>
	<ul class="list-disc">
		<li>
			<strong>consent</strong> to send the newsletter—you can withdraw it whenever
			you like by unsubscribing;
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>legitimate interests</strong> to respond to messages, prevent spam,
			and operate and secure these small websites; and
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>legal obligations</strong> where I am required to keep or disclose information
			by law.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h3>Who receives your data?</h3>
	<p>
		I do not sell personal data. Depending on how you use the site, information
		may be processed by the site's hosting provider, Google (Gmail and
		reCAPTCHA), Mailchimp, YouTube, or Buy Me a Coffee. These providers may
		process data outside the UK under the safeguards described in their own
		privacy notices:
	</p>
	<ul class="list-disc">
		<li>
			<a class="text-link" href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google</a>
		</li>
		<li>
			<a class="text-link" href="https://mailchimp.com/legal/privacy/"
				>Mailchimp</a
			>
		</li>
		<li>
			<a
				class="text-link"
				href="https://www.youtube.com/howyoutubeworks/user-settings/privacy/"
				>YouTube</a
			>
		</li>
		<li>
			<a class="text-link" href="https://www.buymeacoffee.com/privacy-policy"
				>Buy Me a Coffee</a
			>
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h3>How your data is protected</h3>
	<p>
		The services I use are protected with strong passwords and two-factor
		authentication where available. I have chosen established providers which
		say they use appropriate security measures—though of course I have no way of
		personally inspecting Google's servers to verify this. If I discover a
		problem, I will take appropriate action.
	</p>

	<h3>How long I keep information</h3>
	<ul class="list-disc">
		<li>
			Newsletter details are kept until you unsubscribe or I close the mailing
			list, subject to any short suppression record needed to ensure you are not
			resubscribed accidentally.
		</li>
		<li>
			I keep emails while they remain useful for the conversation,
			administration, or any legal requirement. In practice I keep most emails,
			though I delete some when I no longer need them (or need to free up
			space).
		</li>
		<li>
			Any third-party services retain information according to their own
			policies.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h3>Your rights</h3>
	<p>
		As I understand it, UK data-protection law may give you rights to access,
		correct, delete, restrict, or object to the use of your personal data, or to
		receive a copy. In practice, this just means you can have copies of emails
		you have sent me. Feel free to ask.
	</p>
	<p>
		Email{' '}<strong>neil@walkingoncustard.com</strong> and I will do my best to
		help. Yes, asking you to email me to find out what email data I have about you
		seems a little silly, but I can't think of a way around it. I may need to confirm
		your identity first, though.
	</p>
	<p>
		You also have the right to withdraw consent at any time, which in practice
		means unsubscribing from my newsletter. Links are available in every
		message, or email me for help.
	</p>

	<h3>Changes to this notice</h3>
	<p>
		Last reviewed: <strong>31 July 2026</strong>. I will update this page when
		the site changes materially in a way which affects the collection of
		personal data.
	</p>

	<h3>Wasn't that fun?</h3>
	<p>
		Not going to lie, I am glad to have reached the end of writing this. If you
		read the whole thing, I hope you enjoyed it, and I am pleased that you are
		so easily entertained <span role="img" aria-label="smile">🙂</span> Love!
	</p>
</div>

